> ## Consolidated vision: a capability-based execution system
> 
> The core abstraction is no longer a static DAG or merely a selected model. It is a **selected execution strategy**: a versioned, policy-governed way to turn a user turn and available capabilities into an answer and durable artifacts.
> 
> A strategy may be as simple as “call model X directly,” or as sophisticated as “ask model X to write and execute a bounded JavaScript program using search, memory, calculator, and model-call capabilities.”
> 
> ### Terminology
> 
> | Old term | Proposed term | Meaning |
> |---|---|---|
> | Plan | **ExecutionStrategy** | A selectable, versioned approach to solving a request. |
> | PlanSpec | `StrategySpec` | Registration/configuration of a strategy family. |
> | Responder | **StrategyExecutor** | The runtime interface that executes a strategy. |
> | Workflow DAG | **Execution program** | A concrete, per-turn program created or selected by a strategy. |
> | Operation | **Invocation** | A model/tool call or other durable unit of work. |
> | Tool result | **Artifact** | Typed, durable output with provenance. |
> 
> “Plan” can remain as informal UI language, but `ExecutionStrategy` is more precise in code: it captures both selection policy and the method of execution.
> 
> ## Execution strategies
> 
> Initially, support a small set of strategy families:
> 
> 1. **DirectStrategy**  
>    One model call; no tools.
> 
> 2. **ConfiguredStrategy**  
>    A fixed prompt/tool arrangement, such as memory retrieval plus synthesis.
> 
> 3. **ProgramStrategy**  
>    A model generates JavaScript which orchestrates allowed capabilities dynamically.
> 
> 4. **ReviewStrategy**  
>    A draft, verification, and revision process—possibly itself implemented as JavaScript.
> 
> The selector chooses among *strategies and their configurations*, not arbitrary JavaScript programs. The generated JavaScript is an execution artifact of a selected strategy, not itself the long-lived learned policy unit.
> 
> ## JavaScript replaces DAGs as the orchestration language
> 
> A DAG is a useful execution shape, but not the user-facing or policy-facing abstraction. JavaScript can express:
> 
> - conditional branches;
> - loops and iterative research;
> - parallel tool calls via `Promise.all`;
> - adaptive retries;
> - intermediate model calls;
> - dynamic tool parameters;
> - state/artifact inspection.
> 
> The runtime should still record the resulting invocation graph, dependencies, artifacts, and events. In other words:
> 
> > JavaScript is the authoring/orchestration format; the event and artifact graph is the audit and scheduling representation.
> 
> Do not retain a separate hand-authored workflow engine as a competing programming model.
> 
> ## Safe runtime boundary
> 
> Generated JavaScript runs in a sandbox with explicit capabilities, e.g.:
> 
> ```js
> const sources = await tools.search({ query, maxResults: 5 });
> const memory = await tools.memory.retrieve({ query, k: 8 });
> 
> const answer = await models.call({
>   role: "synthesizer",
>   messages: context.withArtifacts([sources, memory]),
> });
> return answer;
> ```
> 
> It must not receive filesystem, network, environment, unrestricted `eval`, or arbitrary process access. Every capability call should create typed artifacts, metrics, provenance, and an event record.
> 
> ## Retain the durable-state architecture
> 
> Keep the strongest parts of the original design:
> 
> - immutable canonical transcript;
> - versioned conversation/project/user state;
> - typed artifacts and source excerpts;
> - append-only event history;
> - explicit feedback attribution to `ResponseRecord`;
> - observed cost, latency, reliability, and quality evidence;
> - visible async updates only through explicit delivery policy.
> 
> Async work becomes suspended/resumable program execution rather than a separate DAG subsystem.
> 
> ## Suggested package direction
> 
> Replace `workflow/` gradually with:
> 
> ```text
> planner/
>   strategies.py        # StrategySpec and StrategyExecutor
>   programs/
>     runtime.py         # sandboxed JS execution
>     capabilities.py    # tool/model/state/artifact APIs
>     events.py          # invocation/event records
>     scheduler.py       # resume delayed/background programs
> ```
> 
> The central loop becomes:
> 
> ```text
> assemble context
> → select ExecutionStrategy
> → persist ResponseRecord
> → execute strategy
> → optionally generate/run bounded JS
> → persist artifacts, metrics, and provenance
> → deliver response or schedule continuation
> ```
> 
> This is more flexible than a fixed DAG while preserving the auditability, safety, and learnability that motivated the DAG design.
